{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790400698,"nonce":"QnWWkeaoR9MK1ZXuU6pbN6nD7fE=","title":"Several results across the bridge: Lua's multiple returns, Python's tuples","message":"A call into Lua asked for one result (zlc_pcall with nresults 1), so `return a, b`, `nil, \"message\"` and string.unpack's position were dropped silently, and a caller in Haxe or Wren had no way to receive more. Lua's rule is that the caller decides how many results it keeps; across the bridge the caller cannot say, so the function's declared results (LuaLS `---@return`) decide.\n\nDesign: a core tuple (caribou::data::tuple_new), several named values as one, with TypeRef::Tuple naming them in the interface. Each language takes it in its own form: Haxe an anonymous structure typed from the annotation, Wren a List, Python a tuple, Lua that many results.\n\nStep 1 (Lua to Haxe and Wren): a Lua function declared with several results is published with a Tuple result; its proxy's call keeps that many results (nil-padded) and gives the tuple. Ash makes a tuple an anonymous object (hlp_alloc_dynobj, typed slots for scalars), the macro types it as a TAnonymous, Wren makes a List.\n\nStep 2 (zyntax languages both ways): zyntax carries several values as a tuple box; a foreign call or method that returns a core tuple gives the program that many values (Foreign::values/value, expanded in zb_foreign_call and zb_foreign_invoke), so Lua gets multiple results and Python a tuple; a zyntax tuple crossing out becomes a core tuple, and Python's tuple return types publish as Tuple. zyntax side is zyntax git-bug 3afee89.","files":null}]}